    "content": "# Atomic Mass, Molecules & Polyatomic Ions\n\n## 1. Learning Objectives\n- Understand Class 9 CBSE chemistry principles of Atomic Mass & Ionic Species.\n- Analyze kinetic state conversions, solution concentrations, and separation methods.\n- Master chemical formula criss-cross rules, atomic mass calculations, and valencies.\n- Evaluate subatomic atomic models (Rutherford, Bohr), isotopes, and octet rules.\n\n## 2. Introduction\nIn Class 9 Chemistry, **Atomic Mass & Ionic Species** examines kinetic particulate matter, mixture separation technologies, chemical stoichiometry laws, valency rules, and subatomic atomic structures. Atomic Mass Unit ($1\\text{ u} = \\frac{1}{12}\\text{ mass of } C-12$), IUPAC element symbols, Molecules of elements ($O_2, N_2, P_4, S_8$) vs compounds ($H_2O, NH_3, CO_2$), Ions (Cations $Na^+, Ca^{2+}$ vs Anions $Cl^-, O^{2-}$), Polyatomic ions ($NH_4^+, SO_4^{2-}, CO_3^{2-}, NO_3^-$).\n\n## 3. Detailed Concept Explanation\nThe study of **Atomic Mass & Ionic Species** equips students with laboratory separation skills, concentration calculation formulas ($\\% = \\frac{\\text{Solute}}{\\text{Solution}} \\times 100$), valency criss-cross formula writing, and Bohr-Bury electronic configurations ($2n^2$).\n\n### Key Chemical Concepts\n1. **Kinetic Matter & Separation Technologies:** Latent heat of fusion/vaporization, Tyndall Effect in colloids, and fractional distillation.\n2. **Stoichiometry & Formula Writing:** Law of conservation of mass, IUPAC atomic mass units ($u$), polyatomic ion valencies, and criss-cross formula balancing ($Al_2(SO_4)_3$).\n3. **Atomic Architecture & Isotopes:** Rutherford $\\alpha$-scattering nucleus discovery, Bohr discrete energy shells (K,L,M,N), valency determination ($8-n$), and isotope applications (Uranium-235 nuclear fuel, Iodine-131 goitre treatment).\n\nMastering **Atomic Mass & Ionic Species** requires understanding chemical laws, executing solute concentration calculations, applying valency criss-cross steps, and drawing Bohr atomic energy shell diagrams.\n\n### Systematic Chemistry Analytical Approach\n1. State given chemical elements, valencies, or mixture components.\n2. Select the appropriate stoichiometry law, concentration formula, or separation technique.\n3. Perform step-by-step chemical calculations or criss-cross ionic balancing.\n4. Output verified chemical formulas, valencies, or percentage concentrations.\n\n## 4. Important Definitions\n- **Latent Heat of Vaporization:** The heat energy required to change $1\\text{ kg}$ of a liquid into gas at atmospheric pressure at its boiling point.\n- **Tyndall Effect:** The scattering of a beam of light by colloidal particles, making the path of light visible.\n- **Valency:** The combining capacity of an atom of an element to form chemical bonds by gaining, losing, or sharing electrons to achieve a stable octet.\n\n## 5. Key Concepts\n- Concentration of Solution Mass $%$ $= \\frac{\\text{Mass of Solute}}{\\text{Mass of Solution}} \\times 100$. (Remember: Solution = Solute + Solvent!).\n- Law of Conservation of Mass: Mass can neither be created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction.\n- Rutherford's gold foil experiment proved that most of the atom is empty space and concentrated positive charge resides in a tiny nucleus.\n\n## 6. Examples\n### Example 1: Solute Concentration Calculation\n*Solution:* A solution contains $40\\text{ g}$ of common salt ($NaCl$) dissolved in $320\\text{ g}$ of water. Calculate mass percentage.\nMass of Solute $= 40\\text{ g}$, Mass of Solvent $= 320\\text{ g}$. Mass of Solution $= 40 + 320 = 360\\text{ g}$.\n$$\\text{Mass } \\% = \\frac{40}{360} \\times 100 = 11.11\\%$$\n\n### Example 2: Writing Chemical Formula of Aluminium Sulphate\n*Solution:* Symbols: $Al$ and $SO_4$. Valencies: $Al = 3+$, $SO_4 = 2-$.\nCriss-crossing valencies: $Al$ gets 2, $SO_4$ gets 3 $\\implies$ Formula is $Al_2(SO_4)_3$.\n\n### Example 3: Electronic Configuration & Valency of Aluminium ($Z=13$)\n*Solution:* Atomic number $Z = 13$. Shell distribution: K=2, L=8, M=3. Valence electrons $= 3$. Since valence electrons $\\le 4$, Valency $= 3$.\n\n## 7. Step-by-Step Explanation\n### Stoichiometric & Experimental Procedure\n1. State given chemical symbols, atomic numbers ($Z$), or mass quantities.\n2. Apply valency rules or concentration equations.\n3. Calculate molecular mass or electronic shell distribution ($2n^2$).\n4. Output verified chemical conclusions.\n\n## 8. Formulas / Rules\n- **Solution Concentration Percentage:**\n  $$\\text{Mass } \\% = \\frac{\\text{Mass of Solute}}{\\text{Mass of Solute} + \\text{Mass of Solvent}} \\times 100$$\n- **Maximum Electrons in $n$-th Energy Shell (Bohr-Bury Rule):**\n  $$\\text{Max Electrons} = 2n^2, \\quad [K(n=1)=2, L(n=2)=8, M(n=3)=18, N(n=4)=32]$$\n- **Valency Calculation Rule:**\n  $$\\text{If Valence } e^- \\le 4 \\implies \\text{Valency} = e^-; \\quad \\text{If Valence } e^- > 4 \\implies \\text{Valency} = 8 - e^-$$\n\n## 9. Worked Problems / Activities\n### Worked Problem 1\nCalculate the molecular mass of Nitric Acid ($HNO_3$). (Atomic masses: $H=1\\text{ u}, N=14\\text{ u}, O=16\\text{ u}$).\n*Solution:* Molecular mass $= (1 \\times 1) + (1 \\times 14) + (3 \\times 16) = 1 + 14 + 48 = 63\\text{ u}$.\n\n### Worked Problem 2\nDifferentiate between Isotopes and Isobars with examples.\n*Solution:* 1. **Isotopes** have the same atomic number ($Z$) but different mass numbers ($A$). Example: $^{12}_6C$ and $^{14}_6C$. 2. **Isobars** have different atomic numbers ($Z$) but the same mass number ($A$). Example: $^{40}_{20}Ca$ and $^{40}_{18}Ar$.\n\n## 10. Try Yourself\n1. Name the separation technique used to separate butter from curd or cream from milk.\n2. Write the chemical formula of Calcium Phosphate ($Ca^{2+}$ and $PO_4^{3-}$).\n\n## 11. Common Mistakes\n- Dividing by mass of solvent instead of mass of total solution when calculating concentration %.\n- Forgetting parentheses around polyatomic ions when multiplying subscripts ($Al_2(SO_4)_3$, not $Al_2SO_{43}$!).\n\n## 12. Quick Revision & Key Takeaways\n- Mass $%$ = $\\frac{\\text{Solute}}{\\text{Solution}} \\times 100$.\n- Colloids show Tyndall Effect.\n- Law of Conservation of Mass: Mass Reactants = Mass Products.\n- Criss-cross valency for formulas ($Al_2(SO_4)_3$).\n- Bohr shells: K=2, L=8, M=18, N=32.\n- Isotopes = Same $Z$, Different $A$; Isobars = Same $A$, Different $Z$."
